{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "toolu_018xeHvS1mqro8QFw9aZaZvd", "type": "tool_result", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\"excerpt_removed\": true, \"references\": [{\"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lean_(proof_assistant)\"}, {\"url\": \"https://los.cs.unibuc.ro/seminar-Lean.html\"}, {\"url\": \"https://graphsearch.epfl.ch/concept/62889984\"}, {\"url\": \"https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/?p=916020\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/breck7/pldb/blob/main/concepts/lean.scroll\"}, {\"url\": \"https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/publication/the-lean-theorem-prover-system-description/\"}, {\"url\": \"https://lean-lang.org/doc/reference/4.34.0/Introduction/\"}, {\"url\": \"https://lean-lang.org/doc/reference/4.32.1/Introduction/\"}, {\"url\": \"https://isaim2016.cs.ou.edu/papers/ISAIM2016_Proofs_DeMoura.pdf\"}, {\"url\": \"https://wiki.krisyotam.com/content/wikipedia_en_all_nopic_2026-03/Lean_4\"}]}"}]}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-20T13:05:42.328Z", "tool_use_result": {"query": "Lean theorem prover launched 2013 Microsoft Research Leonardo de Moura", "results": [{"tool_use_id": "srvtoolu_011rBg3NCLm8gtddskxGFnNx", "content": [{"title": "Lean (proof assistant)", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lean_(proof_assistant)"}, {"title": "Lean is a functional programming language that", "url": "https://los.cs.unibuc.ro/seminar-Lean.html"}, {"title": "graphsearch.epfl.ch", "url": "https://graphsearch.epfl.ch/concept/62889984"}, {"title": "The Lean Theorem Prover (System Description)", "url": "https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/?p=916020"}, {"title": "github.com", "url": "https://github.com/breck7/pldb/blob/main/concepts/lean.scroll"}, {"title": "The Lean Theorem Prover (System Description)", "url": "https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/publication/the-lean-theorem-prover-system-description/"}, {"title": "The Lean Language Reference", "url": "https://lean-lang.org/doc/reference/4.34.0/Introduction/"}, {"title": "The Lean Language Reference", "url": "https://lean-lang.org/doc/reference/4.32.1/Introduction/"}, {"title": "ISAIM2016 Proofs DeMoura", "url": "https://isaim2016.cs.ou.edu/papers/ISAIM2016_Proofs_DeMoura.pdf"}, {"title": "Lean (proof assistant)", "url": "https://wiki.krisyotam.com/content/wikipedia_en_all_nopic_2026-03/Lean_4"}]}, "Lean was launched in 2013 by Leonardo de Moura while he worked at Microsoft Research, and the project has since grown well beyond that origin.\n\n- **Origin:** One source says Leonardo de Moura launched the Lean project when he was at Microsoft Research in 2013, and Lean 0.1 was officially released on June 16, 2014.\n- **Early versions:** Initial versions of the language, later known as Lean 1 and 2, were experimental, and the first moderately stable release was Lean 3 in January 2017 (Lean 3 (first released Jan 20, 2017) was the first moderately stable version of Lean.).\n- **Lean 4:** In 2021, Leonardo de Moura and Sebastian Ullrich released Lean 4: a reimplementation of the Lean theorem prover within itself. Lean 4 is not backwards-compatible with Lean 3.\n- **Later organization:** De Moura and co-founder Sebastian Ullrich launched the Lean Focused Research Organization (FRO) nonprofit in July of 2023.\n- **Affiliation:** De Moura later moved to Amazon Web Services, per Leonardo de Moura while employed by Microsoft Research and now Amazon Web Services.\n\nNote that the search results did not give a single definitive launch date beyond 2013; the 2014 date refers to the first official release."], "durationSeconds": 5.666249541999999, "searchCount": 1}, "tool_result_meta": [{"id": "toolu_018xeHvS1mqro8QFw9aZaZvd", "permission_decision": {"decision": "accept", "source": "hook", "reason_type": "hook"}}]}
